{"id":"1a418096-d771-4f55-ab01-ce66e39cfb6e","object":"chat.completion","created":1785033051,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"import json\\ndef solve(case):\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in case or \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields\\\")\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get(\\\"parent\\\")\\n        if p and p in children:\\n            children[p].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    for node in sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"])):\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if not node.get(\\\"removable\\\", False):\\n            continue\\n        if node[\\\"symbol\\\"] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        remaining_syms = {node_map[rid][\\\"symbol\\\"] for rid in retained if rid != nid}\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_syms):\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed.append(nid)\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(retained)\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) based on the problem description. The task: SE-PE-01, implementing grammar_guided_node_reduction. The payload contains nodes (tree with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility) and required_symbols. The algorithm: Similar to Perses syntax-guided reduction. We need to decide which nodes to remove and which to retain, given constraints: root and required symbols must be preserved; nodes with retained children cannot be removed; removable flag; deeper nodes considered first; tie-break by node ID. Also must ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still present in retained nodes. Also need to consider \"grammar_valid\" but no grammar provided; maybe it's implicit from parent-child relationships? Possibly we need to ensure that after deletion, the tree still obeys some grammar constraints? The description mentions \"grammar valid\" but not explicitly defined. However, from typical Perses, it's about reducing while preserving interestingness (i.e., test case still fails). But here interestingness is abstract: we have \"utility\" but not clear how to use it. The problem statement: \"while preserving an interestingness oracle\". In the payload, there is \"utility\" for each node. Possibly we need to keep nodes with utility > something? Or maybe utility is used for ordering? The description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" That's about symbol presence. Also \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That's clear.\n\nBut what about \"grammar_valid\"? Perhaps the tree must remain a valid parse tree under some grammar. Since we don't have grammar rules, we must assume that any parent-child relation as given is valid. Deleting a node may disconnect its children? In typical tree reduction, we can replace a subtree with a child? The problem says \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\". Possibly we can either delete a node (if removable) and replace it with its single child (if any), or delete a node and its children? Need to interpret.\n\nFrom the examples? Not provided. But we have three fixtures. We need to infer behavior. Could be that each node is a symbol; required_symbols are symbols that must be present. The goal is to remove as many nodes as possible (or reduce utility?) while ensuring that all required symbols are still present in the tree. Also nodes with removable=false cannot be removed. Also \"deeper nodes before shallower\" means we process nodes in order of decreasing depth, then by ID as tiebreak. For each node, if it's removable, not root, not required symbol (but required_symbols are symbols, not node IDs; the node's symbol may be required), and if all its children are also being removed? Or children must be retained if we remove parent? Wait: \"node with retained children\" cannot be removed. So if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it. So we must decide removal order such that children are removed before parent.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Build tree structure: map parent id -> list of children, map node id -> node dict.\n- Determine retained set initially: all nodes (maybe eventually some removed). But we need to iteratively remove nodes subject to constraints.\n- Since we need to return removed_node_ids (in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain).\n- Process nodes in order: sort all nodes by depth descending, then by node ID (alphabetical? The IDs are strings like \"n1\", \"root\". We can sort strings normally). For each node in that order:\n   - Check if it can be removed:\n        - node[\"removable\"] == True\n        - node[\"symbol\"] not in required_symbols (or if node's symbol is required, cannot remove that node even if removable? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Required symbol refers to symbol required, not node ID. So if a node's symbol is in required_symbols, we cannot remove it.)\n        - node[\"id\"] != \"root\"? But root has symbol \"root\", which is probably required. Anyway.\n        - Check that all children of this node are already removed (or will be removed? Since we process in depth order, children are deeper, so they have been processed before parent. If children are retained, we cannot remove parent. That means we should only consider removal if all children are in removed set.)\n        - Check that after removal, the set of symbols from remaining nodes still contains all required_symbols. But if we remove this node, its symbol is removed, but maybe its children have same symbol? However, if we remove the node, its children are also removed? In tree reduction, removing a node typically removes the node and its subtree? The description: \"Retained children\" suggests that children might be retained if we don't remove them. But if we remove a node, do its children become orphans? Usually in tree reduction, you either delete a node and replace with its single child (if available) or delete entire subtree. The \"replacement candidates\" phrase suggests we can replace a node with one of its children (i.e., prune). But the description: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" This implies when we remove a node, its children might still be present (if they are retained). But if we remove a node, its children would be disconnected from the root, they would become unreachable. So to keep them, we need to reattach them to the parent. But there's no reattachment logic given. Likely, we are only allowed to remove leaf nodes? But then \"deeper nodes before shallower\" and \"children retained\" make sense: if a node has children that are retained, we cannot remove it because that would disconnect them. So removal is only allowed for nodes whose entire subtree has been removed (i.e., leaves first). That is typical in tree pruning: you can only prune a node if you also prune all its descendants. So the algorithm: start with all nodes as retained. Process nodes in depth descending order. For each node, if it is removable, not required symbol, and all its descendants are already removed (i.e., its children are not retained), then we can remove it, provided that the set of symbols in the retained set after removal still contains all required symbols. But if we remove a node, its symbol is lost; if that