problem says \"stable ties\" using node ID. So ignore utility.\n\nAlso \"registered_candidate_order\" refers to the order we process? We already define order.\n\nTest on first fixture? Let's manually simulate first case (b-000). Nodes:\nroot (depth0, removable false, symbol root -> required)\nn1 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1 -> required)\nn2 (depth1, removable true, symbol s2)\nn3 (depth1, removable false, symbol s0)\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1)\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2)\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0)\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1.\n\nDepth descending: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6. Sort by id: n4, n5, n6.\nn4: removable true, symbol s1 is required (since s1 is required), so skip because symbol required? Actually s1 is required symbol, but n4 has symbol s1. The node has symbol that is required, so cannot remove it. So skip.\nn5: removable false, skip.\nn6: removable true, symbol s0 not required. Children? n6 has no children (it's leaf). So all children are not in retained (trivially). Check remaining symbols: remove n6, retained set includes root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Symbols: root, s1, s2, s0, s1, s2 -> still root and s1 present. So can remove n6. Remove n6. retained now {root,n1,n2,n3,n4,n5}. removed_order=[n6].\n\nNext depth1: n1,n2,n3 sorted by id: n1(\"n1\"), n2(\"n2\"), n3(\"n3\").\nn1: removable false, skip.\nn2: removable true, symbol s2 not required. Children: n4 is child. Is n4 in retained? Yes, n4 still retained. So cannot remove n2 because child retained. So skip.\nn3: removable false, skip.\n\ndepth0: root, not removable, skip.\n\nSo final retained: root,n1,n2,n3,n4,n5. removed: n6. But expected? Not given. Let's check other fixtures quickly.\n\nSecond case (b-001): Nodes similar but utilities different. n6 (parent n1, symbol s0, removable true). n4 (parent n2, symbol s1, removable true). n5 (parent n3, symbol s2, removable false). Required symbols: root, s1. Process depth2: n4 (s1 required, skip), n5 (removable false), n6 (s0 not required, leaf, removable true, can remove? After removal, symbols: root, n1(s1), n2(s2), n3(s0), n4(s1), n5(s2). Still have s1 from n1 and n4. So remove n6. Then depth1: n1 removable false, n2 removable true, but child n4 retained, so cannot remove; n3 false. So same result: only n6 removed.\n\nThird case similar pattern. So all examples have only one removable leaf (n6) that is not required symbol and its removal doesn't remove any required symbol. The other removable node n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so cannot remove. So output would be removed_node_ids=[\"n6\"], retained_node_ids sorted list of all other IDs. That seems plausible.\n\nBut there could be cases where multiple removals possible. The algorithm handles.\n\nNow we need to output Python source for solve(case) that implements this logic. Use only standard library, no external deps. Must return JSON-serializable dict. Case argument is payload dict.\n\nEdge cases: if case is invalid (e.g., missing required_symbols, nodes not array), raise ValueError.\n\nWe need to follow the strict output contract: return {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}.\n\nNow produce the final implementation string as JSON.\n\nWrite solve(case) function.\n\nImplementation details:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if \"nodes\" not in case or \"required_symbols\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = case[\"required_symbols\"]\n    # Build data structures\n    node_map = {}\n    children_map = {}\n    for node in nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        node_map[nid] = node\n        children_map[nid] = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        pid = node.get(\"parent\")\n        if pid is not None and pid in children_map:\n            children_map[pid].append(node[\"id\"])\n    # Initially retain all\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed_order = []\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        # Conditions for removal\n        if not node.get(\"removable\", False):\n            continue\n        # Cannot remove if node's symbol is required\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # Cannot remove if any child is still retained\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        if any(child in retained for child in children):\n            continue\n        # Check symbols after removal\n        # Compute symbols of remaining nodes (excluding this node)\n        remaining_symbols = set()\n        for rid in retained:\n            if rid == nid:\n                continue\n            remaining_symbols.add(node_map[rid][\"symbol\"])\n        if not all(sym in remaining_symbols for sym in required_symbols):\n            continue\n        # All conditions satisfied: remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    # Prepare output\n    removed_node_ids = removed_order  # list in removal order\n    retained_node_ids = sorted(retained)  # sorted string\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids\n    }\n\nCheck: we use node.get(\"removable\") but in fixtures it's boolean. Good.\n\nNow we must ensure that we treat root and required symbols correctly. The root has removable false, and symbol root is likely in required_symbols, so it's already covered. Also \"never remove a node with retained children\" covered.\n\nPotential nuance: If a node's symbol is required but there are multiple nodes with that symbol, removing one might still leave another node with that symbol. In our check, we check if all required symbols are present after removal. That allows removal if another node with same symbol remains. That seems correct per \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" \"a required symbol\" could be interpreted as any node whose symbol is required? But if there are multiple, removing one possibly allowed. The phrase \"a required symbol\" ambiguous. However, from typical Perses, you cannot remove a node that produces a required symbol if that is the only source? But they state \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". That implies you cannot remove a node that is a required symbol, i.e., the node's symbol is required, regardless of duplicates. But then the condition \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" suggests that if there are duplicates, removal might be allowed. There's a conflict. I'll re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" The phrasing \"a required symbol\" could mean any node that has a required symbol. But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be redundant if you never remove a required symbol. However, the latter is a separate check for when you attempt removal. So the interpretation: \"Never remove ... a required symbol\" means you cannot remove a node that has a symbol that is in the required_symbols list, even if there are other nodes with that symbol. Because it says \"a required symbol\" as a property of the node. That is stronger. But then the \"only if all required symbols remain represented\" would be automatically satisfied if we never remove a required symbol? Not exactly, because you could remove non-required symbols and still lose required symbols if they are only present in nodes that are children of removed nodes? But if you never remove required symbol nodes, you cannot lose them. So the second condition is for non-required symbol nodes. So in our code, we should not remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. That matches the fixture: n4 has symbol s1 which is required, so we didn't remove it. But in a scenario where there are two nodes with same required symbol, would we allow removal of one? According to \"Never remove a required symbol\", no. But the problem statement says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" That suggests never remove any node that is a required symbol. So we must skip all such nodes. That aligns with our earlier reasoning. So we need to check if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols and skip. Not based on counting.\n\nThus our algorithm is correct.\n\nNow produce final answer. The output must be JSON with single key \"implementation\" containing the Python source code as a string.\n\nWe must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Use triple quotes? But it's JSON string, so we need to escape properly. Best to write the code and then encode as JSON manually or with Python's json.dumps. Since we are outputting raw text, we can write the JSON directly, ensuring escaping.\n\nWe'll write a compact version of the code.\n\nAlso include comments? Not necessary, but can. But ensure no unescaped characters.\n\nI'll produce the implementation string:\n\nimport json\ndef solve(case):\n    # validate\n    if \"nodes\" not in case or \"required_symbols\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    # build maps\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get(\"parent\")\n        if p and p in children:\n            children[p].append(n[\"id\"])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # sort by depth desc, then id\n    for node in sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"])):\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        if not node.get(\"removable\", False):\n            continue\n        if node[\"symbol\"] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        if any(c in retained for c in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # check required symbols still present\n        remaining_syms = {node_map[rid][\"symbol\"] for rid in retained if rid != nid}\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(remaining_syms):\n            continue\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        removed.append(nid)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained)\n    }\n\nBut we must ensure that if required_symbols set contains \"root\", then root's symbol is \"root\", so it's in required_symbols, so it will be skipped. Good.\n\nNow we need to wrap in JSON.\n\nResponse must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". Value is the Python source as a string.\n\nSo final output: {\"implementation\":\"import json\\ndef solve(case):\\n    ... \"}\n\nMake sure no extra spaces.\n\nLet's write it."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":4676,"total_tokens":6848,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4315},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}